lesson physics-energy-at-the-water-wheel v=1 format=teach grade=6 unit=4 subject=physics levels=[easy, medium, hard] {
  title "Energy at the water wheel"
  goal "Follow energy from store to store at the water wheel, in joules, knowing that what goes in all comes out."

  look {
    scene 24x24 {
      waterwheel w paddles=8 lit=0 at=canvas(1, 0)
      energyflow e thing="water wheel" from="moving water" to="turning millstone" input=1000 useful=600 wasted=[398, 2] wastes=["heating", "sound"] blank=-1 unit=J below=w gap=1
    }
    say """
      Energy is measured in joules (J). Water held high behind the weir has energy in its
      gravitational store; as it falls it moves, and the moving water pushes the wheel round, so the
      energy is passed by a force into the kinetic store of the wheel. Energy is never made or lost,
      only moved: of each 1000 J here, 600 J turn the millstone and 400 J are wasted, nearly all of
      it warming the mill and its air, and only a couple of joules as sound.
      """
  }
  do {
    practice physics.store-which count=1 seed=1 easy-seed=2 hard-seed=3
    level easy {
      worked physics.store-falling-water m=2 h=3 level=medium
    }
    practice physics.store-falling-water count=2 seed=1 easy-seed=2 hard-seed=3
    practice physics.store-missing count=2 seed=1 easy-seed=2 hard-seed=3
    practice physics.store-efficiency count=1 seed=1 easy-seed=2 hard-seed=3
    practice physics.store-height-back count=1 seed=1 easy-seed=2 hard-seed=3
    practice physics.store-ends-up count=1
  }
  story {
    show physics.store-flour-sack m=20 h=3 easy-m=10 hard-m=25 hard-h=4
  }
  try stars=2 {
    show physics.store-more-out o=1200 easy-o=1500 hard-o=1100
  }
  try stars=3 {
    show physics.store-water-needed v=1 easy-v=0 hard-v=3
  }
  remember {
    say "Energy is never made or lost: the joules going in all come out, some useful and the rest wasted."
  }

  grown-ups """
    The idea to listen for is energy being "used up". Children say a machine uses energy, and the
    lesson keeps asking where it went instead: into turning, into warmth, into sound, and in the
    end into a slightly warmer mill. Watch that the band diagrams are read as a sum, the bands out
    adding to the band in, and that the falling water's joules come from its weight in newtons,
    not its kilograms. If the joules from falling water stall the child, work one bucket aloud,
    weight first and then times the height, and stay with the first questions another day. Off
    the page, lift a 1 kg bag of sugar from the floor onto a table about 1 m high: that is roughly
    10 J into its gravitational store. The three-star question is worked backwards from the
    millstone: first the water's joules, then its kilograms.
    """
  level easy {
    grown-ups "At this level a worked example finds the joules from a small bucket, the missing band is the band in or the useful one, the efficiency is out of 200 J, the sack is 10 kg, and the three-star question takes a 60 in 100 wheel and 900 J."
  }
  level hard {
    grown-ups "At this level the buckets are heavier and fall further, the missing band is a wasted one, the efficiency is out of 800 J, the stores to name are thermal, chemical and elastic, the claim is only a little over, and the three-star wheel keeps half of what it gets."
  }
}
